findings.recommendation:
The row rule, app-wide: A CONTROL ROW IS EVEN OR IT IS STACKED — NEVER WRAPPED. Implement as a grid, never `flex-wrap`:

.gg-suggest-actions {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--btn-gap);
  margin: 8px 0 4px;
}
.gg-suggest-actions > .gg-suggest-btn-neutral { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
.gg-suggest-btn-yes, .gg-suggest-btn-no, .gg-suggest-btn-neutral { justify-content: center; }

That gives two equal verdict pills whose outer edges land exactly on the card's edges, then one full-width deferral beneath — no ragged edge anywhere, and the geometry now states the real structure (two verdicts + one deferral), which is form following function rather than a layout accident.

ANSWERING PAUL'S ACTUAL ASK ('cleaner when they're all in one row'): a true 3-up is not reachable at 390px. Three even cells give 106px each; 'I haven't looked' needs ~136px at her 14px floor. Getting there costs either type below the floor or every label wrapped to two lines. If he wants the literal single row anyway, the only version I would ship is: 3-up even grid, ✓/× moved ABOVE the label instead of inline, `line-height:1.2`, `padding:8px 6px`, `min-height:52px` — ⚠️ but that alters the FORM of the ratified affirmative component (fill and glyph survive, placement changes), so it needs Paul's explicit nod. My recommendation is the 2+1 above, which needs no change to a Paul-stated component and reads as deliberate rather than as a compromise.

Collapse the two cascades into one `weatherReads()` returning a RANKED LIST of `{key, tier, observation, action}` where `key` ∈ {storm, rain-now, saturated, muggy, frost, heat, rain-today, rain-tomorrow, damp-morning, garden-window, baseline} and `tier` ∈ {severe, warning, opportunity, calm}. Then:
  • the glance takes `reads[0]` — the top-ranked read, which is what it already is;
  • 'Worth knowing' renders only reads at tier severe|warning whose `key` DIFFERS from `reads[0].key` — so a condition is stated once, at the highest altitude it earns, and never twice;
  • the promoted severe fire-weather alert (7/14 F5) keeps its own path and is unaffected.
This is a merge, not a rewrite: both cascades' rule bodies survive, they just stop being two functions that each decide what today is. Keep the existing thresholds; where the two disagree (0.02 vs 0.05 rain rate, 0.5 vs 0.75 saturation), take the LOWER — the alert cascade's — since it is the one that already earns a place on the surface.

Restore the subject deterministically — no model call, this is a lookup. In the muggy rule, resolve the named susceptible plants against canon and name only those actually on the property:
  const FUNGAL_SUSCEPTIBLE = ['boxwood', 'hydrangea', 'mountain-laurel'];
  const at = PLANTS_DATA.filter(p => FUNGAL_SUSCEPTIBLE.includes(p.id)).map(p => p.name);
  action: at.length
    ? "Fungal weather for the " + at[0].toLowerCase() + " and anything crowded — water at the base, not the leaves."
    : "Fungal weather — water at the base, not the leaves.";
Two properties worth keeping: it degrades honestly if the plant is later removed from canon, and 'the leaves' beats 'foliage' for this reader. If the list ever needs to be per-plant rather than hardcoded, the right home is a `fungalSusceptible: true` marker in `plants.json` — but do not build that until a second advisory needs it.

Two lines make overflow structurally impossible, and one more keeps the content legible inside the narrower cell:

.rain-byday { grid-template-columns: repeat(7, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
.rain-byday-cell { min-width: 0; }
.rain-byday-day { letter-spacing: 0; }

`minmax(0,1fr)` removes the min-content floor so the tracks always divide the container; `min-width:0` does the same at the item level. Then, because at A+ the 42px demand still exceeds the ~39px track, give the A+ reader full-size numbers on two rows rather than clipped ones on one:

@media (max-width: 480px) {
  body.text-lg .rain-byday { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}

4 + 3 wraps cleanly and preserves left-to-right chronology within each row. Apply the same `minmax(0,1fr)` + `min-width:0` hardening to `.garden-hero-rain-grid` and `.rain-ctx-cols` while you are in there — they are not overflowing today by my arithmetic, but they are the same construction and the same one-long-label failure is one label away. VERIFY BY TOGGLING, not by reading: 390px, A+ on and off, with a 7-day set that includes a two-decimal value and a '—'.
